{"schemaVersion":1,"id":"CVE-2025-4382","state":"PUBLISHED","summary":"A flaw was found in systems utilizing LUKS-encrypted disks with GRUB configured for TPM-based auto-decryption. When GRUB is set to automatically decrypt disks using keys stored in the TPM, it reads the decryption key…","description":"A flaw was found in systems utilizing LUKS-encrypted disks with GRUB configured for TPM-based auto-decryption. When GRUB is set to automatically decrypt disks using keys stored in the TPM, it reads the decryption key into system memory. If an attacker with physical access can corrupt the underlying filesystem superblock, GRUB will fail to locate a valid filesystem and enter rescue mode. At this point, the disk is already decrypted, and the decryption key remains loaded in system memory. This scenario may allow an attacker with physical access to access the unencrypted data without any further authentication, thereby compromising data confidentiality. Furthermore, the ability to force this state through filesystem corruption also presents a data integrity concern.","published":"2025-05-09T11:59:33.176Z","lastModified":"2026-09-01T11:51:10.074Z","withdrawn":null,"severity":{"overall":"MEDIUM","cvss":5.9},"cvss":{"v2":null,"v3_0":null,"v3_1":{"vectorString":"CVSS:3.1/AV:P/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:N","baseScore":5.9,"baseSeverity":"MEDIUM","exploitabilityScore":null,"impactScore":null,"source":"cve.org"},"v4_0":null},"cwe":[{"cweId":"CWE-306","name":"Missing Authentication for Critical Function","source":"cve.org"}],"affected":[{"vendor":"Unknown","product":"Unknown","cpe":null,"versions":[{"version":null,"status":"affected","versionEndExcluding":null,"versionEndIncluding":"2.12"}],"platforms":[]},{"vendor":"Red Hat","product":"Red Hat Enterprise Linux 10","cpe":"cpe:/o:redhat:enterprise_linux:10","versions":[],"platforms":[]},{"vendor":"Red Hat","product":"Red Hat Enterprise Linux 7","cpe":"cpe:/o:redhat:enterprise_linux:7","versions":[],"platforms":[]},{"vendor":"Red Hat","product":"Red Hat Enterprise Linux 8","cpe":"cpe:/o:redhat:enterprise_linux:8","versions":[],"platforms":[]},{"vendor":"Red Hat","product":"Red Hat Enterprise Linux 9","cpe":"cpe:/o:redhat:enterprise_linux:9","versions":[],"platforms":[]},{"vendor":"Red Hat","product":"Red Hat OpenShift Container Platform 4","cpe":"cpe:/a:redhat:openshift:4","versions":[],"platforms":[]},{"vendor":"Red Hat","product":"Red Hat OpenShift Container Platform 4","cpe":"cpe:/a:redhat:openshift:4","versions":[],"platforms":[]}],"configurations":[],"references":[{"url":"https://access.redhat.com/security/cve/CVE-2025-4382","source":"cve.org","tags":["vdb-entry","x_refsource_REDHAT"]},{"url":"https://bugzilla.redhat.com/show_bug.cgi?id=2364416","source":"cve.org","tags":["issue-tracking","x_refsource_REDHAT"]},{"url":"https://gitweb.git.savannah.gnu.org/gitweb/?p=grub.git;a=blobdiff;f=grub-core/kern/rescue_reader.c;h=a71ada8fb7da2eae6ee7135fe234fb1755ca78b0;hp=4259857ba9eea45446bc40ea13c3de4ab1b88ffd;hb=c448f511e74cb7c776b314fcb7943f98d3f22b6d;hpb=4abac0ad5a7914dd3cdfff08aaac06588bf98d80","source":"cve.org","tags":[]}],"exploit":null,"credits":[],"sources":["cve.org"],"primarySource":"cve.org"}
{"schemaVersion":1,"id":"CVE-2025-4598","state":"PUBLISHED","summary":"A vulnerability was found in systemd-coredump. This flaw allows an attacker to force a SUID process to crash and replace it with a non-SUID binary to access the original's privileged process coredump, allowing the att…","description":"A vulnerability was found in systemd-coredump. This flaw allows an attacker to force a SUID process to crash and replace it with a non-SUID binary to access the original's privileged process coredump, allowing the attacker to read sensitive data, such as /etc/shadow content, loaded by the original process.\n\nA SUID binary or process has a special type of permission, which allows the process to run with the file owner's permissions, regardless of the user executing the binary. This allows the process to access more restricted data than unprivileged users or processes would be able to. An attacker can leverage this flaw by forcing a SUID process to crash and force the Linux kernel to recycle the process PID before systemd-coredump can analyze the /proc/pid/auxv file. If the attacker wins the race condition, they gain access to the original's SUID process coredump file. They can read sensitive content loaded into memory by the original binary, affecting data confidentiality.","published":"2025-05-30T13:13:26.049Z","lastModified":"2026-09-01T11:51:15.041Z","withdrawn":null,"severity":{"overall":"MEDIUM","cvss":4.7},"cvss":{"v2":null,"v3_0":null,"v3_1":{"vectorString":"CVSS:3.1/AV:L/AC:H/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:N/A:N","baseScore":4.7,"baseSeverity":"MEDIUM","exploitabilityScore":null,"impactScore":null,"source":"cve.org"},"v4_0":null},"cwe":[{"cweId":"CWE-364","name":"Signal Handler Race Condition","source":"cve.org"}],"affected":[{"vendor":"Unknown","product":"Unknown","cpe":null,"versions":[{"version":null,"status":"affected","versionEndExcluding":"252.37","versionEndIncluding":null},{"version":"253.0","status":"affected","versionEndExcluding":"253.32","versionEndIncludin